<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dromahair</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dromahair is a village in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-leitrim">County Leitrim</a>, Ireland, on the River Bonet near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lough-gill">Lough Gill</a>. It was the main seat of the O'Rourke lords of Breifne and later West Breifne, and in 1152 it was the scene of the abduction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbforgaill">Derbforgaill</a>, wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigernan-ua-ruairc">Tigernán Ua Ruairc</a>, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diarmait-mac-murchada">Diarmait Mac Murchada</a>, an episode later linked to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-norman-invasion-of-ireland">Norman invasion of Ireland</a>. The ruins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/creevelea-friary">Creevelea Friary</a> lie nearby.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dromahair.jpg" alt="Dromahair" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Oliver Dixon · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Main_Street,_Dromahair_-_geograph.org.uk_-_799301.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
